The distribution constant K_d is defined as the ratio of the solute concentration (or amount) in phase A to that in phase B at equilibrium:

\[
K_d=\frac{C_A}{C_B}=\frac{n_A}{n_B}
\]

Hence  

\[
\frac{n_B}{n_A}= \frac{1}{K_d}= \frac{1}{508}=1.9685\times10^{-3}
\]

The total amount of solute is \(n_{\text{tot}} = n_A+n_B\).  
Dividing the expression above by the total gives the fraction in phase B:

\[
\frac{n_B}{n_{\text{tot}}}= \frac{1/K_d}{1+1/K_d}
     = \frac{1}{K_d+1}
     = \frac{1}{508+1}
     = \frac{1}{509}
     \approx 1.97\times10^{-3}
\]

Thus the fraction of the original solute that ends up in phase B is about \(1.96 \times 10^{-3}\).

