The problem: 50 L of helium at 15 °C (which is 288 K) and a pressure of 100 standard atmospheres. Use the ideal gas law \(PV=nRT\).

First convert the pressure to pascals (or use atm with R = 0.082057 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹).

\[
n = \frac{PV}{RT}
   = \frac{(100\ \text{atm})(50\ \text{L})}{(0.082057\ \text{L·atm·mol}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1})(288\ \text{K})}
   \approx \frac{5000}{23.6}
   \approx 212\ \text{mol}.
\]

Helium’s molar mass \(M\) ≈ 4.00 g mol⁻¹, so the mass \(m = nM\):

\[
m \approx 212\ \text{mol} \times 4.00\ \text{g mol}^{-1}
   \approx 8.5 \times 10^{2}\ \text{g}
   \approx 850\ \text{g}.
\]

The closest listed option is **I) 840 gm**.

