The photon energy is  

\[
E_{\text{ph}} = \frac{hc}{\lambda}= \frac{(6.626\times10^{-34}\,\text{J·s})(3.00\times10^{8}\,\text{m/s})}{58.4\times10^{-9}\,\text{m}}
               \approx 3.40\times10^{-18}\,\text{J}
\]

Convert to electron‑volts:

\[
E_{\text{ph}} = \frac{3.40\times10^{-18}\,\text{J}}{1.602\times10^{-19}\,\text{J/eV}} \approx 21.2\ \text{eV}
\]

The kinetic energy of the emitted electron is  

\[
K = \tfrac12 m v^2 = \tfrac12 (9.11\times10^{-31}\,\text{kg})(1.59\times10^{6}\,\text{m/s})^{2}
    \approx 1.15\times10^{-18}\,\text{J}
\]

\[
K = \frac{1.15\times10^{-18}}{1.602\times10^{-19}} \approx 7.2\ \text{eV}
\]

The ionization energy (work function) is the photon energy minus the kinetic energy:

\[
\phi = E_{\text{ph}} - K \approx 21.2\ \text{eV} - 7.2\ \text{eV} \approx 14\ \text{eV}
\]

Thus the ionization energy of krypton is about **14 eV**, corresponding to choice **C**.  

